---
import MarketingLayout from '../../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
---

<MarketingLayout
  title="Open Source — kindling | Free MIT-Licensed Writing Software"
  description="No AI writing, no subscription, no cloud lock-in. kindling is MIT-licensed and 100% free. Browse the source, contribute, or write your novel."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <h1>Free and open source. By design.</h1>
    <p class="page-hero-subtext">kindling is MIT licensed. The source code is public. Your data stays on your machine. This isn't a freemium pitch — it's a philosophy.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="pw-label">02 / The reasoning</p>
    <h2>Why we chose open source</h2>
    <p>Writers invest months and years in their manuscripts. The tools they depend on shouldn't disappear behind a failed subscription model, a sunset announcement, or a pivot to AI. kindling is MIT licensed — meaning anyone can inspect, modify, and redistribute the code. If the project ever stopped being maintained, the community could carry it forward. Your work is never locked in.</p>
    <div class="callout">Your words, your data, forever.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section alt">
    <p class="pw-label">03 / The stack</p>
    <h2>Built with tools that last</h2>
    <ul class="stack-list">
      <li><strong>Tauri 2.x</strong> — lightweight desktop framework (your app is ~10MB, not 200MB)</li>
      <li><strong>Rust</strong> — fast, safe, reliable backend</li>
      <li><strong>Svelte 5</strong> — modern, responsive interface</li>
      <li><strong>SQLite</strong> — your projects are single portable database files</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="callout">Your project files are standard SQLite databases. Even without kindling, your data is always accessible.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="pw-label">04 / Your data</p>
    <h2>No accounts. No tracking. No cloud.</h2>
    <p>kindling requires no account and collects no desktop analytics or telemetry. Your manuscripts and references stay in a local database. Writing and editorial review work offline; you choose how to share exported files. Automatic updates and optional feedback submissions use the internet. See the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> for details.</p>
    <p class="pw-note">We plan to offer optional cloud sync as a paid feature in the future. It will always be optional — the core app will always work completely offline and for free.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section alt">
    <p class="pw-label">05 / Contributing</p>
    <h2>Help shape kindling</h2>
    <p>kindling is built by a solo developer who is also a fiction writer. Contributions are welcome — whether that's code, bug reports, feature requests, or just telling other writers about the project.</p>
    <div class="pw-row community-actions">
      <a href="https://github.com/smith-and-web/kindling" class="pw-button" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Repository</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/smith-and-web/kindling/issues" class="pw-button pw-button--secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Issues</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/smith-and-web/kindling/discussions" class="pw-button pw-button--secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Discussions</a>
      <a href="https://discord.gg/g7bkj4kY8w" class="pw-button pw-button--secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Discord Server</a>
    </div>
    <div class="callout">You don't need to be a developer to contribute. Bug reports, feature ideas, and spreading the word all help.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-section">
    <h2>MIT Licensed. Always free. Always yours.</h2>
    <p class="pw-note"><a href="/features/">Explore features</a> · <a href="/compare/">Compare to other writing apps</a></p>
    <div class="download-buttons single closing-action">
      <a href="/download/" class="pw-button" data-cta-location="open_source_bottom">Download kindling — Free</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</MarketingLayout>

<style>
  /* Close the stranded empty band between the short hero and the first
     section, so the whitespace reads as intentional rather than a gap. */
  .page-hero { padding-bottom: var(--space-m); }
  .content-section:first-of-type { padding-top: var(--space-xl); }

  .stack-list {
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--text-body);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .community-actions { margin-block: var(--space-m); }
  .closing-action { margin-top: var(--space-m); }
</style>
